A||||3||JohnnyK||Alternator removal||||1||
Z||000000||JohnnyK||04-29-2000||03:24 PM||||How do I remove the alternator for the 4cyl? I removed the sliding bolt from the top, and took off the pivot nut from the bottom, but It won't come out.. Looks like you have to slide the whole pivot bolt (the bottom) out, but it is impossible to do that? Seems like it's really stuck? Is that what you have to do? ||||reg||1
Z||000001||William Federle||04-29-2000||03:42 PM||||Put the sliding adjustment lock bolt back in loose - just finger tight. Then remove the negative battery wire if you haven't already and remove the plug with two wires in it from the alternator. Then remove the red wire that is attatched to a stud on the back of the alternator (side opposite the pulley). Then get a pan and put it under the oil filter and remove the oil filter. Then swivel the bottom of the alternator out of the way (from underneath the car) so that you can get to the top bolt that holds the bracket onto the engine block. Remove that bolt and the one that secures the bracket to the engine support bracket. Now reach up and support the alternator with your right hand while reaching up with your left hand and removing the loosely screwed in adjustment locking bolt that is now all that holds the alternator up. Now you just have to twist and turn the alternator to get it out the bottom.<p>[This message has been edited by William Federle (edited 04-29-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||JohnnyK||04-30-2000||12:51 PM||||How can you pivot the bottom of the alternator out of the way with that big pivot bolt going through it? Is that supposed to come out but it's really seized in there or something?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||William Federle||04-30-2000||08:06 PM||||You pivit the alternator out of the way after putting the locking screw back in loosley and then removing the pivot bolt. You probably already found it, but in case you didn't, the pivot bolt isn't threaded into the bracket - there's a nut on the other end of it.<P>One more thing I just found out unhappily. If you get a remanufactured alternator, you might want to go to sears and buy a longer bolt to replace the one that locks the alternator in place after the belt is tightened. Also get a lock washer and nut for it. My belt loosened up and so I retightened it and torqued the locking screw a little tighter. It stripped the threads in the alternator. I guess the remanufactured ones aren't as tough as the original ones.<BR>||||reg||1
